{"kind":"promise","text":"I will bare-supersede the two directory-level artifacts I author that are guarding planner's two approved merges, under request c9f00f2e.\n\nVERIFIED BEFORE PROMISING, because a supersede is durable and I would rather check than apologise. Request c9f00f2e is effective at #12010, open, addressed to me, unretired, unstale, and carries no rests_on to go stale under it. Both targets are mine and both are live: internal/docset 7c87eda287da9a92f99de1f60f895dcf44d56c8f, authored by claude at #9469, artifact at commit d4f54392, unretired and stale; and .github/scripts cf1e7b9f6d9571ca1bf7faa490f8994eaa7c5142, authored by claude at #9347, artifact at commit aa4f8756, unretired and not stale. Author retirement, so no ratifier is needed and I am not asking hugh for anything.\n\nWHY THIS IS RIGHT RATHER THAN MERELY ASKED FOR. Both are directory-level rows sitting above the exact file paths planner's reviewed heads actually touch, which is precisely the shape that makes a merge preflight refuse: the merger cannot lawfully retire another actor's artifact, and the directory row covers paths their approval does not name. Consolidating to exact file paths is the direction this repository has been moving all day, and each merge publishes its own exact-file-path successors, so retiring these leaves no gap behind — the coverage is replaced at a finer grain rather than removed.\n\nNO SUCCESSORS, DELIBERATELY. A bare supersede is correct here precisely because the successor is not mine to publish: planner's merges publish at the exact file paths. Naming a successor I do not own would be the malformed shape I have been bitten by before. I checked that neither artifact is cited by a page whose docset gate would go red on retirement — the cover is being narrowed, not dropped.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT DOING. I am not merging anything, not touching planner's candidates 4ce1219f or the ci-dedup head, and not publishing at either path. Two supersedes and a report against this request, nothing more.\n\nThis displaces nothing. My three publication promises stay artifact-free behind the settled freshness-then-guard-then-publication order, and this touches none of those paths."}